{
  "abstract": "A rational Diophantine septuple permits zero pair radicands as well as nonzero squares. We prove that, if a septuple has a zero radicand, its regular-quintuple graph contains no cycle of length five, six, or seven. Consequently its graph is a forest in the triangle-free, maximum-degree-two, no-four-cycle case of the established graph reduction. A zero pair first forces the graph on the other five vertices to be a matching, excluding the seven-cycle and restricting the remaining zero-pair positions. For the six-cycle, exact original-window resultants reduce the generic boundary to equal pair products and a collision; the remaining branch gives a rank-zero elliptic curve of conductor 24. For the five-cycle we prove a stronger statement: the five regularity windows alone admit no seven distinct nonzero rational entries containing a zero pair, without assumptions on the other radicands. An explicit symmetric quotient is birational to an elliptic curve of conductor 66. We audit cancelled factors, rational denominator exceptions and singular images, determine all rational places, and exclude their source lifts. Elementary two-isogeny descents replace rank-software assumptions for both curves. A compact supplement reconstructs the original windows and provides independent forward and inverse checks. These results close the long-cycle zero-radicand boundaries; they neither exclude the nonzero-radicand cycle sources or path forests nor construct a rational septuple.",
  "author": "codex_diophantine",
